8184 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186087792 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348185 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186087792 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348186 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186087792 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348187 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186087805 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348431 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186087810 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348317 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186087841 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348328 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186087841 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348329 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186087843 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348331 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186087843 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348332 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186087843 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348335 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186087848 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348439 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186087849 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348442 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186087850 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348440 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186087851 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348443 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186087872 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348266 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186087872 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348267 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186087873 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348279 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186087873 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348280 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186087873 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348281 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186087873 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348282 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186087879 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348145 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186087879 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348161 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186087880 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348154 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186087883 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348300 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186087883 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348301 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186088357 1068 #edb/ref 17592186086765 13194139533429 17592186045585 false] [17592186088357 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347992 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186088357 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186088357 1071 "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+16y=0.\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186088357 1071 "Find the general solution to the equation\n$\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} + 16y = 0$." 13194139533429 17592186045585 false] [17592186088357 1074 "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+16{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+16) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+16=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe auxiliary equation has pure imaginary roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+16 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-16 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm 4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186088359 1068 #edb/ref 17592186086777 13194139533429 17592186045585 false] [17592186088359 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347976 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186088359 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186088359 1071 "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+26y=0.\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186088359 1071 "Find the general solution to the equation\n$\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} + 2\\frac{dy}{dx} + 26y = 0$." 13194139533429 17592186045585 false] [17592186088359 1074 "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+2\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+26{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +26) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +26=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +26 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +1)-1+26 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2}+25 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2} & =-25 \\\\ \\lambda +1 & =\\pm 5\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-1\\pm 5\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{-x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186088360 1068 #edb/ref 17592186086783 13194139533429 17592186045585 false] [17592186088360 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347870 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186088360 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186088360 1071 "Which of the following parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate a circle of radius $1$ centered at the point $(3,2)?$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186088360 1071 "Which of the following parametric equations, defined for $θ ∈ [0, 2π)$, generate a circle of radius $1$ centered at the point $(3, 2)$?" 13194139533429 17592186045585 false] [17592186088360 1074 "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +a,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +b,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $1$ centered at the point $(3,2).$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186088365 1068 #edb/ref 17592186086813 13194139533429 17592186045585 false] [17592186088365 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347776 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186088365 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186088365 1074 "The correct answer is $T=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ Indeed, consider the standard basis ${i,j}$ of the Euclidean plane ${R}^{2}$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors, as shown below. \n\n![](images/9e/9e322e6ed1ddb8f1d094633e931cd179b7eb9d6f47acd7c7d0809f113df91b22.png)\n\nNow, we can find the images of ${i,j}$ under $T$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =T(i)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=-i \\\\ {j}^{′} & =T(j)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following image:\n\n![](images/49/4993d8586a84e4a40a54edddd8011cd3d516264bfffc7e3c33d27a10edf1e7d7.png)" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186088368 1068 #edb/ref 17592186086831 13194139533429 17592186045585 false] [17592186088368 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347872 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186088368 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186088368 1071 "![](images/dd/dd128890a13c0aa79d293b70063eeb8b78e07f56dd42774699d1b2f32e43dd0d.png)\n\nThe semi-circle above is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$x = 7\\cos θ, y = 7\\sin θ$.\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $θ$?" 13194139533429 17592186045585 false] [17592186088368 1071 "![](images/dd/dd128890a13c0aa79d293b70063eeb8b78e07f56dd42774699d1b2f32e43dd0d.png)\n\nThe semi-circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186088368 1074 "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=7.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a semi-circle, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/25/25503e83f9a196cab4ded2fd93b09028b1608bd7d815edd538564e1aa611ca86.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\pi ]$, we get the required semi-circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the semi-circle are \n\n$$\nx=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186088369 1068 #edb/ref 17592186086837 13194139533429 17592186045585 false] [17592186088369 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347779 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186088369 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186088369 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+9y=3,\\,y(0)=\\frac{4}{3},\\text{ }\\text{ }{y}^{′}(0)=5.\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186088369 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n$y^{″} + 6y^{′} + 9y = 3, y(0) = \\frac{4}{3}, y^{′}(0) = 5$." 13194139533429 17592186045585 false] [17592186088369 1074 "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+9y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +9 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-3$ is a repeated root, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-3x}+Bx{e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}=0,\\,{y}_{p}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+0+9\\alpha =3\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}+Bx{e}^{-3x}+\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nNow let's find the constants $A$ and $B.$ \n\nSubstituting $y(0)=\\frac{4}{3}$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nA+\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{4}{3}.\n$$\n\nThus, $A=1.$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=5.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(B-3A){e}^{-3x}-3Bx{e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=5$ into the above gives\n\n$$\nB-3A=5.\n$$\n\nSo we have $A=1$ and $B=8.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-3x}+8x{e}^{-3x}+\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186088370 1068 #edb/ref 17592186086843 13194139533429 17592186045585 false] [17592186088370 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347702 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186088370 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186088370 1071 "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+8\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+16y=32x+16.\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186088370 1071 "Find the general solution to the equation\n$\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} + 8\\frac{dy}{dx} + 16y = 32x + 16$." 13194139533429 17592186045585 false] [17592186088370 1074 "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+8\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+16y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +16 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +4)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =4$ is a repeated root, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-4x}+Bx{e}^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=0\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation. This gives:\n\n$$\n0+8\\alpha +16(\\alpha x+\\beta )=32x+16\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n16\\alpha x+(8\\alpha +16\\beta )=32x+16\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}16\\alpha =32\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 8\\alpha +16\\beta =16\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =2,\\,\\beta =0.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=2x.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-4x}+Bx{e}^{-4x}+2x.\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186088371 1068 #edb/ref 17592186086849 13194139533429 17592186045585 false] [17592186088371 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347865 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186088371 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186088371 1071 "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-10\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+25y=25{x}^{2}+5x-3.\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186088371 1071 "Find the general solution to the equation\n$\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} - 10\\frac{dy}{dx} + 25y = 25x^{2} + 5x - 3$." 13194139533429 17592186045585 false] [17592186088371 1074 "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-10\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+25y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +25 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -5)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =5$ is a repeated root, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{5x}+Bx{e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=2\\alpha \n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation. This gives:\n\n$$\n2\\alpha -10(2\\alpha x+\\beta )+25(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=25{x}^{2}+5x-3\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n25\\alpha {x}^{2}+(25\\beta -20\\alpha )x+(2\\alpha -10\\beta +25\\gamma )=25{x}^{2}+5x-3\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}25\\alpha =25\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 25\\beta -20\\alpha =5\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\alpha -10\\beta +25\\gamma =-3\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =1,\\beta =1,\\gamma =\\frac{1}{5}.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={x}^{2}+x+\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{5x}+Bx{e}^{5x}+{x}^{2}+x+\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186088373 1068 #edb/ref 17592186086861 13194139533429 17592186045585 false] [17592186088373 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347630 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186088373 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186088373 1071 "Consider the matrices\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 7 & 6\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -7 & 6\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 7 & -6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $AB=BA={I}_{4}$\n- $AC=CA={I}_{4}$\n- $