da }^{2}+2\\lambda +26 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +1)-1+26 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2}+25 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2} & =-25 \\\\ \\lambda +1 & =\\pm 5\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-1\\pm 5\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-1\\pm 5\\text{i}$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)={e}^{-x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x).\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral, ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $, and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}=2\\alpha x+\\beta ,\\,{y}_{p}^{″}=2\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation. This gives\n\n$$\n2\\alpha +2(2\\alpha x+\\beta )+26(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=13{x}^{2}+15x-24.\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n26\\alpha {x}^{2}+(26\\beta +4\\alpha )x+(2\\alpha +2\\beta +26\\gamma )=13{x}^{2}+15x-24\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}26\\alpha =13\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 26\\beta +4\\alpha =15\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\alpha +2\\beta +26\\gamma =-24\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{1}{2},\\beta =\\frac{1}{2},\\gamma =-1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{x}{2}-1.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{x}{2}-1.\n$$\n\nNow let's find the constants $P$ and $Q.$  \n\nSubstituting $y(0)=2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nP-1=2.\n$$\n\nThus, $P=3.$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=\\frac{5}{2}.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={e}^{-x}[(5Q-P)\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x-(5P+Q)\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x]+x+\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=\\frac{5}{2}$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n5Q-P+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo we have $P=3$ and $Q=1.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-x}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{x}{2}-1.\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186088388 1068 #edb/ref 17592186086951 13194139533429 17592186045585 false] [17592186088388 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347709 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186088388 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186088388 1071 "Find the standard matrix that  represents  the  linear  transformation $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x-3y+8z \\\\ x+z+2y\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186088388 1071 "Find the standard matrix that represents the linear transformation $T: R^{3} → R^{2}$, where\n$T: \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x - 3y + 8z \\\\ x + z + 2y\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139533429 17592186045585 false] [17592186088388 1074 "We require a $2\\times 3$ matrix $T$ such that\n\n$$\nT\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1x+(-3)y+8z \\\\ 1x+2y+1z\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 8 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x-3y+8z \\\\ x+z+2y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ma