13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186101544 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=2x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2y}{x}-2 \\\\ 2 & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2\\cdot 2x}{x}-2 \\\\ 2 & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4x}{x}-2 \\\\ 2 & \\overset{?}{=}4-2 \\\\ 2 & =2.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=2x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y={x}^{2},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2y}{x}-2 \\\\ 2x & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2\\cdot {x}^{2}}{x}-2 \\\\ 2x & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{x}-2 \\\\ 2x & \\overset{?}{=}2x-2 \\\\ 0 & \\ne -2.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *false*. Therefore, the function $y={x}^{2}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y={x}^{3},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2y}{x}-2 \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2\\cdot {x}^{3}}{x}-2 \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{x}-2 \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & \\overset{?}{=}2{x}^{2}-2 \\\\ {x}^{2} & \\ne -2\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y={x}^{3}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186101545 1068 #edb/ref 17592186100401 13194139533429 17592186045585 false] [17592186101545 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347681 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186101545 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186101545 1071 "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $(\\frac{1}{xy} + \\frac{1}{y})\\frac{dy}{dx} = 1$ that satisfies $y(1) = e$." 13194139533429 17592186045585 false] [17592186101545 1071 "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $(\\frac{1}{xy}+\\frac{1}{y})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=1$ that satisfies $y(1)=e.$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186101545 1074 "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{1}{xy}+\\frac{1}{y})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}(\\frac{1+x}{x})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{x}{x+1} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{x}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int (1-\\frac{1}{x+1})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & =x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the natural logarithm to both sides, we find the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|} & ={e}^{x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C} \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C} \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{x}\\cdot {e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|}\\cdot {e}^{C} \\\\ y & =\\frac{K{e}^{x}}{x+1},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is an arbitrary constant. \n\nWe now apply the initial condition $y(1)=e$ to find $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & =e \\\\ \\frac{K{e}^{1}}{1+1} & =e \\\\ K & =2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the particular solution of $(\\frac{1}{xy}+\\frac{1}{y})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=1$ that satisfies the condition $y(1)=e$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2{e}^{x}}{x+1}\\,.\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186105706 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348068 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186105715 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348157 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186105716 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348158 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186106495 1068 #edb/ref 17592186105088 13194139533429 17592186045585 false] [17592186106495 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347656 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186106495 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186106495 1071 "A linear transformation $T$ represents a stretch along the $x$-axis or $y$-axis. Given that $v = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}$ and $T(v) = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$, what is the standard matrix of $T$?" 13194139533429 17592186045585 false] [17592186106495 1071 "A linear transformation $T$ represents a stretch along the $x$-axis or $y$-axis. Given that $v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]$ and $T(v)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],$ what is the standard matrix of $T?$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186106495 1074 "First, we are told that $T$ represents a stretch along the $x$-axis or $y$-axis.\n\nComparing the $x$- and $y$-components of $v$ and $T(v),$ we note the following:\n\n- the $x$-components of $v$ and $T(v)$ are the same, while\n\n- the $y$-component of $T(v)$ is $\\frac{1}{3}$ times the $y$-component of $v.$\n\nSo, we conclude that $T$ represents a stretch of scale factor $\\frac{1}{3}$ in the $y$-direction.\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix of $T$ is\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186115235 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348094 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186115235 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348095 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186115236 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348108 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186115261 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348117 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186115261 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348118 1