132b7bd02cb64f864b6982c0c64a66c22646334ca199fad12097ba435.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 2{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{2{x}^{2}}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186147436 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348236 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186147437 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348237 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186147444 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348323 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186147445 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348324 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186147445 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348325 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186147446 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348326 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186147450 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348238 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186147452 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348246 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186147456 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348247 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186147456 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348250 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186147457 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348251 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186147514 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348231 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186147515 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348232 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186147597 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348249 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186147598 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348248 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186148208 1068 #edb/ref 17592186146972 13194139533429 17592186045585 false] [17592186148208 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347708 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186148208 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186148208 1071 "Find the inverse Laplace transform for $F(s) = \\frac{1}{s - 5}$." 13194139533429 17592186045585 false] [17592186148208 1071 "Find the inverse Laplace transform for $F(s)=\\frac{1}{s-5}.$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186148208 1074 "From the table of Laplace transforms, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{5t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-5},\\,s>5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{5t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-5} \\\\ {L}^{-1}{L{{e}^{5t}}} & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s-5}} \\\\ {e}^{5t} & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s-5}} \\\\ {L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s-5}} & ={e}^{5t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186152193 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348198 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186152194 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348225 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186152195 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348199 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186152195 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348200 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186152196 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348194 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186152196 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348195 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186152196 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348196 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186152196 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348197 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186152198 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348284 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186152198 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348291 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186152198 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348302 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186152199 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348285 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186152199 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348286 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186152199 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348287 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186152199 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348303 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186152199 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348304 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186152199 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348305 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186152204 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348416 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186152205 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348405 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186152206 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348418 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186152206 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348419 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186152284 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348314 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186152284 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348315 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186152284 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348316 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186152291 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348073 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186152293 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348137 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186152297 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348318 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186152298 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348319 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186152298 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348320 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186152299 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348321 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186152299 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348322 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186152803 1068 #edb/ref 17592186151295 13194139533429 17592186045585 false] [17592186152803 1068 #edb/ref 17592186348011 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186152803 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186152803 1071 "![](images/e5/e5f0d56383e7cdc7bece094bb3a770695bf87f6d2bf6c4dce5876f4432b56803.png)\n\nThe quarter-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$x = 3\\cos θ, y = 2\\sin θ$.\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $θ$?" 13194139533429 17592186045585 false] [17592186152803 1071 "![](images/e5/e5f0d56383e7cdc7bece094bb3a770695bf87f6d2bf6c4dce5876f4432b56803.png)\n\nThe quarter-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186152803 1074 "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=3$ and $b=2.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the elipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/0b/0b38ee461b8895898a73f2b2120e1dac9823b1bf19b46924786c5c27c9beae74.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$, we get the quarter-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186152809 1068 #edb/ref 17592186151331 13194139533429 17592186045585 false] [17592186152809 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347653 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186152809 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186152809 1071 "![](images/4e/4e9218dcfc77ec080139f2650ea19f78199557e0ce2d8d53c1996ec0ea6145a6.png)\n\nThe half-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$x = 3\\cos θ, y = 2\\sin θ$.\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $θ$?" 13194139533429 17592186045585 false] [17592186152809 1071 "![](images/4e/4e9218dcfc77ec080139f2650ea19f78199557e0ce2d8d53c1996ec0ea6145a6.png)\n\nThe half-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186152809 1074 "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=3$ and $b=2.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a half-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the elipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/df/dfbe843a9ad0847d7174984fa7c7c48834af48b3ee2335b5383b15f7fb826763.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[\\pi ,2\\pi ]$, we get the half-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the half-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [\\pi ,2\\pi ].\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186152810 1068 #edb/ref 17592186151337 13194139533429 17592186045585 false] [17592186152810 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347655 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186152810 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186152810 1071 "Calculate the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186152810 1071 "Calculate the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n$x = 4\\cos θ - 1, y = \\sin θ + 1, 0 \\le θ < 2π$." 13194139533429 17592186045585 false] [17592186152810 1074 "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1 \\\\ \\frac{x+1}{4} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{16} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1 \\\\ y-1 & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ (y-1)^{2} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{16}+(y-1)^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{16}+(y-1)^{2}=1.\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186152815 1068 #edb/ref 17592186151367 13194139533429 17592186045585 false] [17592186152815 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347805 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186152815 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186152815 1071 "Given that $y_{1}(x) = \\sin x$ and $y_{2}(x) = \\cos x$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear, second-order ODE, then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n1. $y(x) = (\\sin x\\cos x)^{2}$\n2. $y(x) = x\\sin x\\cos x$\n3. $y(x) = \\sin x + \\cos x$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 false] [17592186152815 1071 "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear, second-order ODE, then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)=(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{2}$\n- $y(x)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$\n- $y(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186152815 1074 "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, then $y(x)={y}_{1}(x)+{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)=(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{2}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a function and the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186152817 1068 #edb/ref 17592186151379 13194139533429 17592186045585 false] [17592186152817 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347841 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186152817 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186152817 1071 "Given that $y_{1}(x) = \\sin (2x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear, second-order ODE, then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n1. $y(x) = \\sin^{2} (2x)$\n2. $y(x) =-\\sin (2x)$\n3. $y(x) = x^{2}\\sin (2x)$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 false] [17592186152817 1071 "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear, second-order ODE, then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)={sin}^{2}⁡(2x)$\n- $y(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$\n- $y(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186152817 1074 "According to the superposition principle, if the function ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c$ is a constant, then $y(x)=c{y}_{1}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)={sin}^{2}⁡(2x)$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution 