 by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & ={60}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & =(\\sqrt{3})^{2}+{3}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡ϕ+{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =3+9 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =12 \\\\ R & =2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)=2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+{60}^{∘})\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348354 1068 #edb/ref 17592186348038 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348354 1069 "q-80147" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348354 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348354 1071 "$2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)≈$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348354 1072 #uuid "b6b79f0a-30fd-5a34-a89c-78169fdf9be8" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348354 1074 "The difference formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)-(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x),$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{4}{2\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\sqrt{2} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{2}) \\\\  & =0.955\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & =(2\\sqrt{2})^{2}+{4}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡ϕ+{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =8+16 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =24 \\\\ R & =2\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)=2\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x-0.955)\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348355 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347907 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348355 1069 "q-80158" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348355 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348355 1071 "![](images/fd/fd233e2f5214cdc5a27cffa20ccb7ddcfc8ff089963c4381363edabd328ace5c.png)\n\nWhich of the following polynomials could be the function shown in the diagram above?" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348355 1072 #uuid "cd8a38b5-776b-5d50-93b5-312fa39b0bc7" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348355 1074 "Let's call $P(x)$ the polynomial shown in the diagram.\n\nThe end behavior of $P(x)$ is\n\n- $P(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to -\\infty ,$ and\n\n- $P(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nConsequently, \n\n- the degree of $P(x)$ is even,\n\n- the leading coefficient of $P(x)$ is negative, and\n\n- the $y$-intercept is $1,$ which means that $P(0)=1.$\n\nTherefore, of the given options, the only one that satisfies all three criteria is $y=-{x}^{6}-{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}-x+1.$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348356 1068 #edb/ref 17592186348046 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348356 1069 "q-80186" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348356 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348356 1071 "![](images/c7/c77f7a71ccb7b4ad1598b09bf4fe585c82a7f614ebb4abe3f495643a746fcb3c.png)\n\nWhich integral gives the area of the finite region bounded by $y=-x$ and $y=2-{x}^{2},$ shown above?\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348356 1072 #uuid "04bc4a80-6413-522f-a0e8-eb2ae79dcbbc" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348356 1074 "The diagram shows that the intersection points between the two graphs occur when $x=-1$ and $x=2.$ So these will be our limits of integration.\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/9e/9e6e1147d95c65465c802e8bfad5c4ac285dc9716d098452c41bdd6caed5b9ca.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=-x,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=2-{x}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}[\\,(2-{x}^{2})-(-x)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}(-{x}^{2}+x+2)\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348357 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347759 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348357 1069 "q-80193" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348357 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348357 1071 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $4=4-\\frac{3\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{2}$ for $-2\\pi <x<0,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348357 1072 #uuid "708ed8ba-e035-53b7-9300-ab76a887742c" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348357 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =4-\\frac{3\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{2} \\\\ 0 & =-\\frac{3\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can't find an equivalent equation involving tangent, since this would lead to us dividing by $0:$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{1}{0}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can instead find an equivalent equation using the fact that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=0\n$$\n\nIn this case, we can find the principal solution as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ lies outside the given domain, it is not a solution.\n\nTo find more solutions, we subtract integer multiples of $\\pi $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =-\\pi +x=-\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{2}=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2} & =-2\\pi +x=-2\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{2}=-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our solutions are $x=-\\frac{3\\pi }{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=(-\\frac{3\\pi }{2})\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\frac{3{\\pi }^{2}}{4}.\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348358 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347798 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348358 1069 "q-80200" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348358 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348358 1071 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $7\\operatorname{cot}⁡x-5=-5-3\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ for $-\\pi <x<\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348358 1072 #uuid "82240314-e152-5274-978a-b5eee6a6b692" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348358 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7\\operatorname{cot}⁡x-5 & =-5-3\\operatorname{cot}⁡x \\\\ 10\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can't find an equivalent equation involving tangent, since this would lead to us dividing by $0:$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{1}{0}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can instead find an equivalent equation using the fact that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=0\n$$\n\nIn this case, we can find the principal solution as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value lies inside the desired domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nTo find more solutions, we subtract integer multiples of $\\pi $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\pi  \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our solutions are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}.\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348359 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347788 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348359 1069 "q-8022" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348359 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348359 1071 "Let \n$T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x+y \\\\ 2y\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $T(kv)=kT(v)$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vector $v$ and real scalar $k$\n- $T({v}_{1}+{v}_{2})=T({v}_{1})+T({v}_{2})$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$\n- $T$ is a linear transformation" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348359 1072 #uuid "00b02bef-cbb1-520c-b74e-661d82b27e5e" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348359 1074 "A linear transformation $T$ satisfies two properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}]=kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}], \\\\  & T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the first condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}kx+ky \\\\ 2(ky)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}k(x+y) \\\\ k(2y)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =k[\\begin{aligned}x+y \\\\ 2y\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the second condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} \\\\ 2({y}_{1}+{y}_{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{y}_{1} \\\\ 2{y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}+{y}_{2} \\\\ 2{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{T}$ is a linear transformation.\n\nAs a result, all three statements are true." 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348360 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347649 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348360 1069 "q-8027" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348360 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348360 1071 "Find the standard matrix that  represents  the  linear  transformation $T$, where\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}2x-y \\\\ x-y\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348360 1072 #uuid "68c48374-0bdd-59bd-ad8a-c986ebcde36e" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348360 1074 "We require a $2\\times 2$ matrix $T$ such that\n    \n\n$$\nT\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot x+(-1)\\cdot y \\\\ 1\\cdot x+(-1)\\cdot y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that\n    \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2x-y \\\\ x-y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n    Therefore, the matrix which represents the linear transformation $T$ is\n    \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348361 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347732 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348361 1069 "q-80300" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348361 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348361 1071 "Which of the numbers below belong to  ${R}_{0}^{+}?$\n\n- $0$\n- $\\sqrt{18}$\n- $-2\\sqrt{5}$\n- $-\\frac{1}{5}$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348361 1072 #uuid "ac020dea-1486-5e82-bd01-29594c8fbf7a" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348361 1074 "Remember that ${R}_{0}^{+}$ is the set of all positive real numbers and $0.$\n\n- $0$ belongs to ${R}_{0}^{+}.\\,✓$\n\n- $\\sqrt{18}=3\\sqrt{2}$ belongs to ${R}_{0}^{+}.\\,✓$\n\n- $-2\\sqrt{5}$ is a negative number, so it does *not* belong to ${R}_{0}^{+}.\\,\\times $\n\n- $-\\frac{1}{5}$ is a negative number, so it does *not* belong to ${R}_{0}^{+}.\\,\\times $\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348362 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347950 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348362 1069 "q-80302" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348362 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348362 1071 "![](images/1f/1f49d3448340c612269cdeea20680d717c736cb9a19fcf83dac86da1e4a6127f.png)\n\nThe graph of a polynomial $p(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=-1$ is a simple root.\n- $x=2$ is a root of odd multiplicity.\n- $x=2$ is a simple root." 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348362 1072 #uuid "5211c5f5-64c4-596b-937a-7f0d7439b0b9" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348362 1074 "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The root $x=-1$ is a simple root because the graph crosses the $x$-axis at the point and the curve is not tangent to the $x$-axis at that point.\n\n- Statement II is true. The root $x=2$ has an odd multiplicity because at $x=2$ the graph passes through the $x$-axis at this point.\n\n- Statement III is false. The root $x=2$ is not a simple root because the graph crosses the $x$-axis at that point, and the curve is tangent to the $x$-axis at that point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348363 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347916 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348363 1069 "q-80303" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348363 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348363 1071 "![](images/0a/0a7b19b50822235a2dcbfcdf74984735a1e26b2b775180b24bdc2bcbf24c588b.png)\n\nThe graph of a polynomial $p(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=-2$ is a root of even multiplicity.\n- $x=1$ is **not** a simple root.\n- $x=1$ is a root of even multiplicity." 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348363 1072 #uuid "9e0f13e6-ba4b-56eb-b269-f07bb6ae2732" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348363 1074 "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The root $x=-2$ has an odd multiplicity because at $x=-2$ the graph passes through the $x$-axis at this point.\n\n- Statement II is true. The root $x=1$ is not a simple root because the graph crosses the $x$-axis at that point, and the curve is tangent to the $x$-axis at that point.\n\n- Statement III is false. The root $x=1$ has an odd multiplicity because at $x=1$ the graph passes through the $x$-axis at this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348364 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347700 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348364 1069 "q-8031" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348364 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348364 1071 "Find the standard matrix that  represents  the  linear  transformation $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{2},$ where\n$T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ x-y-z\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348364 1072 #uuid "fbc34aed-a47a-5af0-97a2-e33cec90cf2e" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348364 1074 "We require a $2\\times 3$ matrix $T$ such that\n    \n\n$$\nT\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0x+0y+0z \\\\ 1x+(-1)y+(-1)z\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that\n    \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ x-y-z\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix which represents the linear transformation $T$ is\n    \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348365 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347767 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348365 1069 "q-80312" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348365 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348365 1071 "$\\int \\frac{2}{|x+1|\\sqrt{(x+1)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348365 1072 #uuid "f140ef15-884a-5848-bea0-7026afe13f1e" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348365 1074 "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2}{|x+1|\\sqrt{(x+1)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int \\frac{1}{|x+1|\\sqrt{(x+1)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse secant:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{arcsec}(u)+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $(x+1).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution \n\n$$\nu=x+1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2}{|x+1|\\sqrt{(x+1)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int \\frac{1}{|x+1|\\sqrt{(x+1)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\,\\text{arcsec}(u)+C \\\\  & =2\\,\\text{arcsec}(x+1)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348366 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347712 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348366 1069 "q-80318" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348366 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348366 1071 "Find an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =6.$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348366 1072 #uuid "1ab0b5f3-c889-5093-b43d-5502efb2f927" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348366 1074 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =6.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]-6[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-5{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n-5{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}={x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348367 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347857 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348367 1069 "q-80321" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348367 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348367 1071 "Find an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -6 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2.$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348367 1072 #uuid "127d67ea-4345-520f-adfc-12e0e0bd714c" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348367 1074 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-2.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -6 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -6 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n-3{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-2{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348368 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347829 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348368 1069 "q-80324" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348368 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348368 1071 "Given that $\\lambda =2$ and $\\lambda =-1$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -3\\end{aligned}]$,\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A.$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348368 1072 #uuid "4d98964f-3df0-5437-bc4a-3bb084da1d43" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348368 1074 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -3\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -5 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{5}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For $\\lambda =-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -3\\end{aligned}]-(-1)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{5}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=5,$ we get the eigenvector\n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348369 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347823 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348369 1069 "q-80327" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348369 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348369 1071 "Given that $\\lambda =1$ and $\\lambda =10$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 7\\end{aligned}]$,\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A.$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348369 1072 #uuid "3ce7471b-9a77-56e2-b53e-fffca538d26a" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348369 1074 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 7\\end{aligned}]-1[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain  ${x}_{1}=-2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For $\\lambda =10,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 7\\end{aligned}]-10[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 6 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-6{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348370 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347766 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348370 1069 "q-80331" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348370 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348370 1071 "Consider the following sets:\n\n$$\nN,\\,Z,\\,Q,\\,R,\\,C\n$$\n\nTo which of these sets does the number $-\\frac{27}{3}$ belong?" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348370 1072 #uuid "39280445-a454-56b4-bdba-1c7ee371d8c7" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348370 1074 "![](images/67/67ef97be4b21a74feaae15f3ff30d5077fc1316874df8de79155316a621e4487.png)\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- $N$ represents the set of **natural numbers**.\n\n- $Z$ represents the set of **integers**.\n\n- $Q$ represents the set of **rational numbers**.\n\n- $R$ represents the set of **real numbers**.\n\n- $C$ represents the set of **complex numbers**\n\nWith that in mind, we note the following:\n\n- The number $-\\frac{27}{3}=-9$ is negative, so it does *not* belong to $N,$ and we can write\n\n$$\n-\\frac{27}{3}\\notin N.\n$$\n\n- However, $-9$ is an integer, so it does belong to $Z,$ and we can write\n\n$$\n-\\frac{27}{3}\\in Z.\n$$\n\n- Every integer is a rational, real, and complex number. Consequently, $-\\frac{27}{3}$ belongs to $Q,R,$ and $C,$ and we can write\n\n$$\n-\\frac{27}{3}\\in Q,\\,-\\frac{27}{3}\\in R,\\,-\\frac{27}{3}\\in C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$Z,Q,R,$ and $C$ only.\"" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348371 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347915 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348371 1069 "q-80332" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348371 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348371 1071 "Consider the following sets:\n\n$$\nN,\\,Z,\\,Q,\\,R,\\,C\n$$\n\nTo which of these sets does the number $\\pi $ belong?" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348371 1072 #uuid "3c87cce5-2762-52a9-b3ea-307f7f887046" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348371 1074 "![](images/67/67ef97be4b21a74feaae15f3ff30d5077fc1316874df8de79155316a621e4487.png)\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- $N$ represents the set of **natural numbers**.\n\n- $Z$ represents the set of **integers**.\n\n- $Q$ represents the set of **rational numbers**.\n\n- $R$ represents the set of **real numbers**.\n\n- $C$ represents the set of **complex numbers**\n\nWith that in mind, we note the following:\n\n- The number $\\pi $ is not an integer, so it does *not* belong to $Z$ nor to $N,$ so we can write\n\n$$\n\\pi \\notin N,\\,\\pi \\notin Z.\n$$\n\n- Also, notice that $\\pi =3.14159\\,…\\,$ can not be written as the ratio of two integers, so it does *not* belong to $Q$ either, and we can write \n\n$$\n\\pi \\notin Q.\n$$\n\n- Every irrational number is both a real number and a complex number. Consequently, $\\pi $ belongs to both $R$ and $C,$ and we can write\n\n$$\n\\pi \\in R,\\,\\pi \\in C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$R$ and $C$ only.\"" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348372 1068 #edb/ref 17592186348052 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348372 1069 "q-80338" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348372 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348372 1071 "Which of the numbers below belong to  ${Z}_{0}^{-}?$\n\n- $-15$\n- $0$\n- $-\\pi $\n- $-\\frac{27}{3}$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348372 1072 #uuid "4eae16cb-f514-5e15-a012-1e904a842a1d" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348372 1074 "Remember that ${Z}_{0}^{-}$ is the set of all negative integer numbers and $0.$\n\n- $-15$ belongs to ${Z}_{0}^{-}.\\,✓$\n\n- $0$ belongs to ${Z}_{0}^{-}.\\,✓$\n\n- $-\\pi $ is not an integer, so it does *not* belong to ${Z}_{0}^{-}.\\,\\times $\n\n- $-\\frac{27}{3}=-9$ belongs to ${Z}_{0}^{-}.\\,✓$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I, II, and IV only\"." 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348373 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347667 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348373 1069 "q-80342" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348373 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348373 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\sqrt{1-z}}.$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348373 1072 #uuid "027c33f1-e979-5d6a-94a5-ba6a2c9a56e5" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348373 1074 "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\sqrt{1-z}} & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\sqrt{1-z}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-2\\sqrt{1-z}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\sqrt{1-0}-\\sqrt{1-a}) \\\\  & =-2+2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }\\sqrt{1-a} \\\\  & =-2+2\\cdot \\infty  \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the integral is divergent." 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348374 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347782 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348374 1069 "q-80347" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348374 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348374 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{8-{x}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348374 1072 #uuid "bc51916f-5f62-552c-9209-e6b4fd87da10" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348374 1074 "First, we substitute $u=8-{x}^{3}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-3{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,-\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u={x}^{2}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=8-{x}^{3}$ is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & -1 & -\\infty  \\\\ u & 9 & \\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{8-{x}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{\\infty }^{9}(-\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{u}})\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{9}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{8-{x}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{9}^{a}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[2\\sqrt{u}]}_{9}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\sqrt{a}-\\sqrt{9}] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\infty  \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the integral is divergent." 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348375 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347886 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348375 1069 "q-80348" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348375 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348375 1071 "![](images/8b/8b086759a25af7e464c0c8be78d36cd4d8458c5c25e7c84cb6ecb28b2d59e9df.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the equation of the polynomial curve shown above?" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348375 1072 #uuid "ad55c8cb-7248-5482-9f28-0afc365f8908" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348375 1074 "First, we notice that the function has precisely three distinct roots, $x=-1$, $x=0$ and $x=2.$\n\nBased on the graph, we can make the following conclusions about the multiplicities of the roots:\n\n- Since the graph is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=-1,$ this root is a simple root (the multiplicity equals $1$).\n\n- Since the graph passes through $x=0,$ this root has odd multiplicity, and since the graph is tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=0,$ the multiplicity must be greater than one.\n\n- Since the graph passes through $x=2,$ this root has odd multiplicity, and since the graph is tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=2,$ the multiplicity must be greater than one.\n\nFrom the given options, the only possible equation is $y={x}^{3}(x+1){(x-2)}^{3}.$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348376 1068 #edb/ref 17592186348024 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348376 1069 "q-80350" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348376 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348376 1071 "![](images/5e/5e350d9b4db36831eac3ff0708a2acbc903c07265d4c6709632fb8a854121747.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the equation of the polynomial curve shown above?" 1319