48383 1069 "q-80396" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348383 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348383 1071 "For the function $f(x,y),$ calculate $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}$ given that $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2xy{e}^{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}.$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348383 1072 #uuid "fa43a83f-a97a-54b7-8060-e07ac7e5d1a6" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348383 1074 "To find $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}$ we differentiate the given first-order partial derivative $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{∂f}{∂x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy{e}^{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}) \\\\  & =2y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{e}^{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}) \\\\  & =2y\\cdot ({e}^{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}+2{x}^{2}{e}^{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}) \\\\  & =2y(1+2{x}^{2}){e}^{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348384 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347899 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348384 1069 "q-80403" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348384 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348384 1071 "Find ${f}_{xx}(x,y)+{f}_{yy}(x,y)$ given that  ${f}_{x}(x,y)=-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348384 1072 #uuid "afef13bf-72ea-50e0-9df2-d85aa9be21e5" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348384 1074 "To find ${f}_{xx}(x,y)$ we differentiate the given first-order partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{x}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-y\\cdot (-\\frac{2x}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2xy}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find ${f}_{yy}(x,y)$ we differentiate the given first-order partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{yy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({f}_{y}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =x\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =x\\cdot (-\\frac{2y}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2xy}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y)+{f}_{yy}(x,y) & =\\frac{2xy}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}-\\frac{2xy}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348385 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347713 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348385 1069 "q-80442" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348385 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348385 1071 "What is the cardinality of $A\\times B$ for $A={0,1,2,3,...,7}$ and $B={a,b,c,d}?$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348385 1072 #uuid "ffef2906-3fbb-534f-bd32-11f9a33b8796" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348385 1074 "For any two finite sets $A$ and $B,$ the cardinality of $A\\times B$ is\n\n$$\n|A\\times B|=|A|\\cdot |B|.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A\\times B| & =|A|\\cdot |B| \\\\  & =8\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348386 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347930 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348386 1069 "q-80447" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348386 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348386 1071 "If $Y={1,2,{{2}},3,2,4,{{∅}},4}$, then $|Y|=$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348386 1072 #uuid "c70367e7-3689-592b-be67-c8b4a8c81e8b" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348386 1074 "The cardinality of a finite set $Y$ is the number of elements in $Y.$ \n\nLet's start by listing all the elements of $Y:$\n\n$$\n1,\\,2,\\,{{2}},\\,3,\\,4,\\,{{∅}}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- Only *unique* elements are counted. In other words, duplicate elements are *not* counted separately.\n\n- ${{2}}$ and ${{∅}}$ each count as single elements of $Y.$\n\nSince $Y$ has $6$ elements, the cardinality is $|Y|=6.$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348387 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347608 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348387 1069 "q-80453" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348387 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348387 1071 "Determine if the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$\nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348387 1072 #uuid "4fad8010-b1d7-51d3-ae7c-0e9c4f636170" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348387 1074 "Since $A$ is triangular, the eigenvalues are the entries on the main diagonal. \n\nSo, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$ Since we get one repeated eigenvalue, the matrix might not be diagonalizable. To check this, we row-reduce the matrix $(A-(-2)I)\\text{x}=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has only $1$ pivot column. This implies that we will have only $2-1=1$ linearly independent eigenvector corresponding to the current eigenvalue. Therefore, $A$ is not diagonalizable." 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348388 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347640 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348388 1069 "q-80454" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348388 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348388 1071 "Determine if the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]$\nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348388 1072 #uuid "082d41d1-cb8b-540c-9c6f-0eb8d16da184" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348388 1074 "First, we find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ -1 & 3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )(3-\\lambda )-4\\cdot (-1) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda -1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=1.$ Since we get one repeated eigenvalue, the matrix might not be diagonalizable. To check this, we row-reduce the matrix $(A-1I)\\text{x}=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-1I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has only $1$ pivot column. This implies that we will have only $2-1=1$ linearly independent eigenvector corresponding to the current eigenvalue. Therefore, $A$ is not diagonalizable." 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348389 1068 #edb/ref 17592186348010 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348389 1069 "q-80455" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348389 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348389 1071 "Determine if the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -12 \\\\ 4 & -7\\end{aligned}]$ \nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348389 1072 #uuid "48f981b1-9aeb-5e6b-818c-36bd836ee49e" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348389 1074 "First, we find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}7-\\lambda  & -12 \\\\ 4 & -7-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (7-\\lambda )(-7-\\lambda )-4\\cdot (-12) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)(\\lambda -1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$ Since we get two distinct eigenvalues, the matrix $A$ is diagonalizable.\n\nNow, we construct the diagonal matrix $D,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348390 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347795 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348390 1069 "q-80456" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348390 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348390 1071 "Find ${f}_{xy}(x,y)+{f}_{yx}(x,y)$ given that  ${f}_{x}(x,y)=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+y}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+y}.$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348390 1072 #uuid "0067d163-3060-585d-9cac-2338577d165d" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348390 1074 "To find ${f}_{xy}(x,y)$ we differentiate the given first-order partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({f}_{x}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+y}) \\\\  & =2x\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+y}) \\\\  & =2x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{({x}^{2}+y)^{2}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2x}{({x}^{2}+y)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find ${f}_{yx}(x,y)$ we differentiate the given first-order partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{y}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+y}) \\\\  & =(-\\frac{1}{({x}^{2}+y)^{2}})\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =-\\frac{2x}{({x}^{2}+y)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y)+{f}_{yx}(x,y) & =-\\frac{2x}{({x}^{2}+y)^{2}}-\\frac{2x}{({x}^{2}+y)^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4x}{({x}^{2}+y)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348391 1068 #edb/ref 17592186348005 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348391 1069 "q-80457" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348391 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348391 1071 "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 6 \\\\ -9 & 7\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & a \\\\ 1 & b\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $D={P}^{-1}AP,$ find the value of $\\frac{a}{b}.$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348391 1072 #uuid "4f9c3543-a306-5635-8fa9-149cba24aebf" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348391 1074 "From the diagonal matrix $D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1.$\n\nSo, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=1.$ \n\nNow, we simply need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=1.$ Computing $A-I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 6 \\\\ -9 & 7\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 6 \\\\ -9 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 6 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=3,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{2}{3}.$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348392 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347758 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348392 1069 "q-80458" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348392 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348392 1071 "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}10 & -12 \\\\ 8 & -10\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & ∗ \\\\ -1 & ∗\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $A=PD{P}^{-1},$ find the second column of the matrix $P.$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348392 1072 #uuid "455fd380-72f1-5a0f-82f0-362abef14cc0" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348392 1074 "From the diagonal matrix $D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2.$\n\nSo, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=2.$ \n\nNow, we simply need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=2.$ Computing $A-2I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & -12 \\\\ 8 & -10\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -12 \\\\ 8 & -12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -12 & 0 \\\\ 8 & -12 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}8 & -12 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348393 1068 #edb/ref 17592186348035 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348393 1069 "q-80462" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348393 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348393 1071 "If ${F}^{′}(t)=⟨3{t}^{2}-5,\\,\\frac{1}{t}⟩,$ then $F(t)=$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348393 1072 #uuid "0ca46b33-5e33-5b0e-9bb0-208be2c3c166" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348393 1074 "First, we note that \n\n$$\nF(t)=\\int {F}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\int ⟨3{t}^{2}-5,\\,\\frac{1}{t}⟩\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTo integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ⟨3{t}^{2}-5,\\,\\frac{1}{t}⟩\\text{d}t & =⟨\\int (3{t}^{2}-5)\\,\\text{d}t,\\int \\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{3}-5t+{c}_{1},\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|+{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{3}-5t,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|⟩+⟨{c}_{1},\\,{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{3}-5t,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|⟩+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary (vector) constant of integration." 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348394 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347909 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348394 1069 "q-80463" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348394 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348394 1071 "If ${F}^{′}(t)=⟨{csc}^{2}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩,$ then $F(t)=$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348394 1072 #uuid "ded3c050-9fa9-5e56-9de6-7af402d33361" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348394 1074 "First, we note that \n\n$$\nF(t)=\\int {F}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\int ⟨{csc}^{2}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTo integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ⟨{csc}^{2}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\text{d}t & =⟨\\int {csc}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t,\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{cot}⁡t+{c}_{1},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{cot}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩+⟨{c}_{1},\\,{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{cot}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary (vector) constant of integration." 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348395 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347832 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348395 1069 "q-80464" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348395 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348395 1071 "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}8 & -12 \\\\ 6 & -10\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & a \\\\ 2 & b\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $D={P}^{-1}AP,$ find the value of $\\frac{a}{b}.$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348395 1072 #uuid "93fc1e8e-53c7-5d4c-ad22-c729f51f630d" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348395 1074 "From the diagonal matrix $D=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2.$ \n\nSo, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=2.$ \n\nNow, we simply need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=2.$ Computing $A-2I,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -12 \\\\ 6 & -10\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -12 \\\\ 6 & -12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -12 \\\\ 6 & -12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}6 & -12 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{2}{1}=2.$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348396 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347666 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348396 1069 "q-80465" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348396 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348396 1071 "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 \\\\ -12 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & a \\\\ -2 & b\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $D={P}^{-1}AP,$ find the value of $\\frac{b}{a}.$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348396 1072 #uuid "cb9e7fcc-e638-502e-8bf0-7fbc40ad862a" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348396 1074 "From the diagonal matrix $D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1.$\n\nSo, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=1.$ \n\nNow, we simply need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=1.$ Computing $A-I,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 \\\\ -12 & 5\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 2 \\\\ -12 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -12 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2}.$  Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=3,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{3}{1}=3.$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348397 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347727 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348397 1069 "q-80468" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348397 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348397 1071 "Find ${f}_{xy}(x,y)+{f}_{yx}(x,y)$ given that  ${f}_{x}(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}).$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348397 1072 #uuid "3a379c4d-6975-5a4f-a1f2-d51e99e08ac7" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348397 1074 "To find ${f}_{xy}(x,y)$ we differentiate the given first-order partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({f}_{x}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})) \\\\  & =x\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\cdot (-2y)) \\\\  & =2xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find ${f}_{yx}(x,y)$ we differentiate the given first-order partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{y}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})) \\\\  & =-y(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\cdot 2x) \\\\  & =2xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y)+{f}_{yx}(x,y) & =2xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})+2xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =4xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348398 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347949 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348398 1069 "q-80469" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348398 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348398 1071 "Find ${g}_{xy}(x,y)+{g}_{xx}(x,y)$ given that  ${g}_{x}(x,y)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348398 1072 #uuid "3cbdca8a-7538-57ef-93a7-e4f6f9084490" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348398 1074 "To find ${g}_{xy}(x,y)$ we differentiate the given first-order partial derivative ${g}_{x}(x,y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({g}_{x}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =x\\cdot (-\\frac{2y}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2xy}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find ${g}_{xx}(x,y)$ we differentiate the given first-order partial derivative ${g}_{x}(x,y)$ with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{xx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({g}_{x}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-\\frac{x}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{xy}(x,y)+{g}_{xx}(x,y) & =-\\frac{2xy}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{2}-2xy-{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348399 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347875 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348399 1069 "q-80474" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348399 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348399 1071 "Calculate ${f}_{yyx}(x,y)$ given that $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy).$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348399 1072 #uuid "09777d76-95e6-5bbb-83f0-f307b490599a" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348399 1074 "First, we find the first-order derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find ${f}_{yy}(x,y)$ by differentiating ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{yy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({f}_{y}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find ${f}_{yyx}(x,y)$ by differentiating ${f}_{yy}(x,y)$ with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{yyx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{yy}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & =-2x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-{x}^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\cdot y \\\\  & =-2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-{x}^{2}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348400 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347757 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348400 1069 "q-80480" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348400 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348400 1071 "Find matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PD{P}^{-1}=E,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix and $E=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 9 \\\\ -6 & -8\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348400 1072 #uuid "050f4d1f-7ad3-50e3-a5b8-1b02cf764a27" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348400 1074 "We apply the diagonalization algorithm. \n\n**Step 1.** We need to find the eigenvalues of the matrix $E$ using its characteristic equation $det(E-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}7-\\lambda  & 9 \\\\ -6 & -8-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (7-\\lambda )(-8-\\lambda )-9\\cdot (-6) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -2 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda +2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues of $E$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $E.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E-I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 9 \\\\ -6 & -8\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 9 \\\\ -6 & -9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 9 & 0 \\\\ -6 & -9 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}6 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-2,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 9 \\\\ -6 & -8\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 9 \\\\ -6 & -6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 9 & 0 \\\\ -6 & -6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & ={R}_{2}+(\\frac{2}{3}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}9 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348401 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347676 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348401 1069 "q-80481" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348401 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348401 1071 "Find matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PD{P}^{-1}=B,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix and $B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348401 1072 #uuid "cac09b37-360a-5efb-9630-b76644cac8f9" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348401 1074 "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.** We need to find the eigenvalues of the matrix $B$ using its characteristic equation $det(B-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ -1 & -2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(-2-\\lambda )-2\\cdot (-1) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda +1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues of $B$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$ \n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $B.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-0I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]-0[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\