 separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}\\frac{{e}^{y}}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{R}(\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}})({e}^{y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & =({\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x)({\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x]_{1}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot [{e}^{y}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\sqrt{3}-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1)\\cdot ({e}^{1}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\cdot (e-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{12}(e-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348410 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347780 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348410 1069 "q-80523" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348410 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348410 1071 "Find the curvature at an arbitrary point on the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by its arc length $s$, where\n\n$$\nr(s)=⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{5}),\\,5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{5})⟩,\\,0\\le s<10\\pi .\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348410 1072 #uuid "19bd97de-146f-5b50-9592-8b78d39231d5" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348410 1074 "Let $T(s)$ denote the unit tangent to the given curve as a function of the arc length $s.$ Since we are given the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by the arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\nκ(s)=‖{T}^{′}(s)‖=‖{r}^{″}(s)‖\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(s)$ and ${r}^{″}(s):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(s) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{5})),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{5}))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{5})\\cdot \\frac{1}{5},\\,5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{5})\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{5}),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{5})⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(s) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{5})),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{5}))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{5})\\cdot \\frac{1}{5},\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{5})\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}⟩ \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{5}),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{5})⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(s) & =‖{r}^{″}(s)‖ \\\\  & =|-\\frac{1}{5}|\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{5}))}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{5}))}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{s}{5})+{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{s}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348411 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347985 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348411 1069 "q-80532" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348411 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348411 1071 "Given that $u=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]$ is a non-zero  vector that is orthogonal to the set $W={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]},$ find the value of $\\frac{a+c}{b}.$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348411 1072 #uuid "5ef98bda-3fda-5602-b92d-821d33ee9d77" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348411 1074 "The vector $u$ is orthogonal to the set $W$ if $u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0$ and $u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0 \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 6{x}_{1}-10{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 0 & -10 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{1}{2} & -1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 0 & -10 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-6){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{1}{2} & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd), so ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=\\frac{4}{3}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{5}{3}{x}_{3}.$ So, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=3,$ we get the vector \n$[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, we obtain $a=5,$ $b=4,$ and $c=3.$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a+c}{b}=\\frac{5+3}{4}=2.$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348412 1068 #edb/ref 17592186348049 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348412 1069 "q-80535" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348412 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348412 1071 "Given that $u=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]$ is a non-zero  vector that is orthogonal to the set $W={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]},$ find the value of $\\frac{c}{a+b}.$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348412 1072 #uuid "381469fe-c30e-5e7c-8c65-89d47cb39659" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348412 1074 "The vector $u$ is orthogonal to the set $W$ if $u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0$ and $u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0 \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-8{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -8 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd), so ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{5}{2}{x}_{3}.$ So, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=2,$ we get the vector \n$[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, we obtain $a=-5,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=2.$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{c}{a+b}=\\frac{2}{-5+(-1)}=-\\frac{1}{3}.$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348413 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347839 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348413 1069 "q-80537" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348413 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348413 1071 "Given that $u=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]$ is a non-zero vector that is orthogonal to the set $W={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]},$ find the value of $\\frac{a+b}{c}.$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348413 1072 #uuid "f12e74f9-0d03-5239-9556-6fe5100d1a38" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348413 1074 "The vector $u$ is orthogonal to the set $W$ if $u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0$ and $u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0 \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -8 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd), so ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-2{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}={x}_{3}.$ So, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{3} \\\\ -2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=1$, we get the vector \n$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, we obtain $a=1,$ $b=-2,$ and $c=1.$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a+b}{c}=\\frac{1+(-2)}{1}=-1.$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348414 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347951 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348414 1069 "q-80538" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348414 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348414 1071 "Given that $u=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]$ is a non-zero vector that is orthogonal to the set $W={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]},$ find the value of $\\frac{b}{a+c}.$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348414 1072 #uuid "e4d3a685-1659-50ba-add8-e425b2e4e04e" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348414 1074 "The vector $u$ is orthogonal to the set $W$ if $u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0$ and $u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0 \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd), so ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{5}{2}{x}_{3}.$ So, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=4$, we get the vector \n$[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, we obtain $a=-10,$ $b=-3,$ and $c=4.$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a+c}=\\frac{-3}{-10+4}=\\frac{1}{2}.$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348415 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347669 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348415 1069 "q-80539" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348415 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348415 1071 "Given that $u=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]$ is a non-zero vector that is orthogonal to the set $W={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]},$ find the value of $\\frac{b+c}{a}.$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348415 1072 #uuid "ff28c518-d713-5726-9541-4836cde2e6b1" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348415 1074 "The vector $u$ is orthogonal to the set $W$ if $u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0$ and $u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0 \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd), so ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-\\frac{2}{5}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{5}{x}_{3}.$ So, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{5}{x}_{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{5}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=5$, we get the vector \n$[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, we obtain $a=-1,$ $b=-2,$ and $c=5.$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b+c}{a}=\\frac{-2+5}{-1}=-3.$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348416 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347764 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348416 1069 "q-80540" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348416 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348416 1071 "![](images/50/506a298041a875549ec60c5cf99ebdbeb5ca4b5717037083fa6ca196fce27279.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If $R=[-2,2]\\times [0,3],$ and\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{2x+y}{y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+y}{y}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin D\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in R \\\\ \\frac{2x+y}{y}, & (x,y)\\in D∖R\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+y}{y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348416 1072 #uuid "3b7813ca-41d9-58f5-aaf1-c07ba10ad235" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348416 1074 "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](images/d2/d2b5c55b15f594680e8f299899d690cd1d92baa1ebef31ce5c79af52afec631e.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $\\frac{2x+y}{y}$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{2x+y}{y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- We cannot use function II. Since $D∖R$ is empty, the function $f(x,y)$ will be identical to zero for all points in $R,$ including $D.$ But $\\frac{2x+y}{y}$ is not zero, for example, at $(x,y)=(0,2)\\in D.$\n\n- Functions I and III equals $\\frac{2x+y}{y}$ on $D$ and equals $0$ outside $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348417 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347959 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348417 1069 "q-80545" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348417 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348417 1071 "Find the curvature at an arbitrary point on the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by its arc length $s,$ where\n\n$$\nr(s)=⟨\\frac{1}{3}(1-2s)^{3/2},\\,\\frac{1}{3}(2s)^{3/2}⟩,\\,0<s<\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348417 1072 #uuid "61dc28ce-9be1-5118-b764-acd32225832e" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348417 1074 "Let $T(s)$ denote the unit tangent to the given curve as a function of the arc length $s.$ Since we are given the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by the arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\nκ(s)=‖{T}^{′}(s)‖=‖{r}^{″}(s)‖\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(s)$ and ${r}^{″}(s):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(s) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{1}{3}(1-2s)^{3/2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{1}{3}(2s)^{3/2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot (1-2s)^{1/2}\\cdot (-2),\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot (2s)^{1/2}\\cdot 2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-(1-2s)^{1/2},\\,(2s)^{1/2}⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(s) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-(1-2s)^{1/2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}((2s)^{1/2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (1-2s)^{-1/2}\\cdot (-2),\\,\\frac{1}{2}(2s)^{-1/2}\\cdot 2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(1-2s)^{-1/2},\\,(2s)^{-1/2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nFinally, the curvature is  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(s) & =‖{r}^{″}(s)‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{((1-2s)^{-1/2})}^{2}+{((2s)^{-1/2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(1-2s)^{-1}+(2s)^{-1}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{1-2s}+\\frac{1}{2s}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2s(1-2s)}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348418 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347973 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348418 1069 "q-80566" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348418 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348418 1071 "![](images/d9/d92fdb93f7564e1c205ab8603945eb6704c03db92c46130810e2b7cbce9a0e20.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+xy & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${∬}_{R}({x}^{2}+xy)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[0,2]\\times [-1,1]$\n- ${∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+xy)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[0,2]\\times [-2,2]$\n- ${∬}_{R}({x}^{2}+xy)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[0,3]\\times [-2,2]$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348418 1072 #uuid "63f1d1b0-1722-552b-83dc-40f0511e40cd" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348418 1074 "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+xy, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+xy)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The rectangle $R$ does not cover the region $D.$ Also, the integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\n- Statement II is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\n- Statement III is false. The integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348419 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347838 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348419 1069 "q-80567" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348419 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348419 1071 "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed between the curve $y={x}^{2}-4$ and the $x$-axis. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}3x-y, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${∬}_{D}(3x-y)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-3,2]\\times [-4,1]$\n- ${∬}_{R}(3x-y)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-3,3]\\times [-4,4]$\n- ${∬}_{D}(3x-y)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [-4,0]$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348419 1072 #uuid "87eecbfe-e80e-5243-96ba-7676172d9f04" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348419 1074 "First, let's draw our region $D:$\n\n![](images/a7/a7af801fa9975dda6ce3b12b2cde2c328721cd5e0affd16f1a14ab26cfa21bdd.png)\n\nNotice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}(3x-y), & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(3x-y)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statements I is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\n- Statement II is false. The integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\n- Statements III is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348420 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347778 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348420 1069 "q-80576" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348420 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348420 1071 "Consider the vector subspace $H=\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}.$ Which of the following vectors belong to ${H}^{⊥}?$\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348420 1072 #uuid "c3a6e8de-174f-5eb2-a5e5-1ab50e3df3b6" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348420 1074 "Let  $h=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ A vector $v$ belongs to ${H}^{⊥}$ if $v$ is orthogonal to $h.$ So, we have to find the products ${v}_{i}\\cdot h$ for $i=1,2,3.$  \n\n- For ${v}_{1}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot h & =1\\cdot (-1)+(-1)\\cdot (-2)+3\\cdot 3=10\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${v}_{1}\\notin {H}^{⊥}.$\n\n- For ${v}_{2}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2}\\cdot h & =(-3)\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot (-2)+(-1)\\cdot 3=-4\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${v}_{2}\\notin {H}^{⊥}.$\n\n- For ${v}_{3}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3}\\cdot h & =(-2)\\cdot (-1)+4\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot 3=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${v}_{3}\\in {H}^{⊥}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"${v}_{3}$ only\"." 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348421 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347918 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348421 1069 "q-80581" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348421 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348421 1071 "Consider the vector subspace $H=\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]}.$ Which of the following vectors belong to ${H}^{⊥}?$\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348421 1072 #uuid "105935bf-05fc-5973-9fd9-74c435a6a9b6" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348421 1074 "Let  $h=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].$ A vector  $v$ belongs to ${H}^{⊥}$ if $v$ is orthogonal to $h.$ So, we have to find the products ${v}_{i}\\cdot h$ for $i=1,2,3.$   \n\n- For ${v}_{1}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot h & =4\\cdot 1+(-5)\\cdot 4+(-4)\\cdot (-4)=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSo, ${v}_{1}\\in {H}^{⊥}.$\n\n- For ${v}_{2}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2}\\cdot h & =2\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 4+(-3)\\cdot (-4)=30\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSo, ${v}_{2}\\notin {H}^{⊥}.$\n\n- For ${v}_{3}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3}\\cdot h & =1\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot 4+4\\cdot (-4)=-19\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${v}_{3}\\notin {H}^{⊥}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"${v}_{1}$  only\"." 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348422 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347854 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348422 1069 "q-80584" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348422 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348422 1071 "Consider the vector subspace $H=\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]}.$ Which of the following vectors belong to ${H}^{⊥}?$ \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348422 1072 #uuid "1238e0cd-699d-501e-95e4-355fa11e8452" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348422 1074 "Let's denote the vectors that span the subspace $H$ as ${h}_{1}$ and ${h}_{2}.$  \n\nNow, a vector $v$ belongs to ${H}^{⊥}$ if $v$ is orthogonal to the set of vectors that spans the subspace $H.$ So, we have to find the products ${v}_{i}\\cdot {h}_{j}$ for $i=1,2,3$ and  $j=1,2.$ \n\n- For ${v}_{1}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {h}_{1} & =3\\cdot (-1)+(-2)\\cdot (-1)+5\\cdot 4=19\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSo, ${v}_{1}\\notin {H}^{⊥}.$ We do not need to test ${v}_{1}\\cdot {h}_{2}.$\n\n- For ${v}_{2}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2}\\cdot {h}_{1} & =3\\cdot (-1)+(-3)\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot 4=8\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${v}_{2}\\notin {H}^{⊥}.$ We do not need to test ${v}_{2}\\cdot {h}_{2}.$\n\n- For ${v}_{3}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3}\\cdot {h}_{1} & =7\\cdot (-1)+5\\cdot (-1)+3\\cdot 4=0 & ✓ \\\\ {v}_{3}\\cdot {h}_{2} & =7\\cdot 2+5\\cdot (-1)+3\\cdot (-3)=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${v}_{3}\\in {H}^{⊥}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"${v}_{3}$ only.\"" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348423 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347652 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348423 1069 "q-80601" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348423 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348423 1071 "Find the general solution to the matrix differential equation $\\,{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]x(t).$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348423 1072 #uuid "2c07c85b-2338-5aaf-86ea-d4180044dfc7" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348423 1074 "Given a decoupled system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\lambda \\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\lambda t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the matrix of our differential equation is diagonal, the system is decoupled. \n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t/2}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t/2},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348424 1068 #edb/ref 17592186348009 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348424 1069 "q-80603" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348424 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348424 1071 "Consider the system of linear differential equations given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}]x(t).\n$$\n\nGiven that the solution to the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ a\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ a\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-2t}+[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$\n\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of the system's matrix is $\\lambda =-2.$*" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348424 1072 #uuid "08ad4637-1a63-55fa-b32f-7dbcbe48ebf5" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348424 1074 "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-2$ (of multiplicity two). For this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution of the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-1+2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3+2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}-{w}_{2}=1 \\\\ {w}_{1}-{w}_{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the first equation is the same as the second. So, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}={w}_{2}+1.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$ we get ${w}_{1}=1,$ and therefore\n\n$$\nw=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-2t}+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, we see that $a=1,b=1,$ and $a+b=2.$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348425 1068 #edb/ref 17592186348034 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348425 1069 "q-80604" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348425 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348425 1071 "Consider the system of linear differential equations given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}]x(t).\n$$\n\nGiven that the solution to the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-t}+[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$\n\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of the system's matrix is $\\lambda =-1.$*" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348425 1072 #uuid "fa98e78d-c213-5ff9-b494-b8ef24528f71" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348425 1074 "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-1$ (of multiplicity two). For this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution of the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1+1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -3+1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{w}_{1}+4{w}_{2}=-2 \\\\ -1{w}_{1}-2{w}_{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the first equation is a constant multiple of the second. So, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=-1-2{w}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$ we get ${w}_{1}=-1,$ and therefore\n\n$$\nw=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-t}+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, we see that $a=-2,b=-1,$ and $a+b=-3.$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348426 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347862 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348426 1069 "q-80607" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348426 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348426 1071 "Consider the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by arc length $s.$ Find the curvature at the point where $s=1$ if the unit tangent vector of the curve is given by\n\n$$\nT(s)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{s}^{2}}}⟨1,s⟩.\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348426 1072 #uuid "0e0662ae-2a01-57b3-bb86-0addfedab9d0" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348426 1074 "Since we are given that the curve $r(s)$ and its unit tangent vector $T(s)$ are parametrized by the arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the curvature: \n\n$$\nκ(s)=‖{T}^{′}(s)‖\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $T(s)$ with respect to $s$ and evaluate it at $s=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{s}^{2}}}⟨1,s⟩) \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{s}^{2}}}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{1+{s}^{2}}})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{s}{(1+{s}^{2})^{3/2}},\\,\\frac{1}{(1+{s}^{2})^{3/2}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(1+{s}^{2})^{3/2}}⟨-s,1⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{(1+{1}^{2})^{3/2}}⟨-1,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}⟨-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we obtain the curvature:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(1) & =‖{T}^{′}(1)‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}‖⟨-1,1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348427 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347895 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348427 1069 "q-80608" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348427 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348427 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of the system's matrix is $\\lambda =1.$*" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348427 1072 #uuid "608190d5-e5cd-5f82-85c0-726eb6ec9bc4" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348427 1074 "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is $\\lambda =1$ (of multiplicity two). For this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3-1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -1-1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{w}_{1}+4{w}_{2}=-2 \\\\ -{w}_{1}-2{w}_{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the first equation is a constant multiple of the second. So, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=-1-2{w}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$  we get ${w}_{1}=-1,$ and therefore\n\n$$\nw=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{t}+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}-2{c}_{1}-{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2{c}_{1}-{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}-([\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{t}+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(2t-1){e}^{t} \\\\ (1-t){e}^{t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348428 1068 #edb/ref 17592186348008 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348428 1069 "q-80614" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348428 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348428 1071 "Find the curvature at an arbitrary point on the curve $r(t)$ given that its derivative ${r}^{′}(t)$ and the derivative of the corresponding unit tangent ${T}^{′}(t)$ are as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)=\\,i-\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}}\\,j,\\,{T}^{′}(t)=-\\frac{t}{2\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}}\\,i-\\frac{1}{2}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348428 1072 #uuid "56077b9c-81fb-5eb2-90a5-cf058b4a5d1a" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348428 1074 "Since we are given both the derivative of the curve ${r}^{′}(t)$ and the derivative of the unit tangent ${T}^{′}(t)$, we will use the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the magnitudes of ${r}^{′}(t)$ and ${T}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{(-\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4-{t}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4-{t}^{2}+{t}^{2}}{4-{t}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}} \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{t}{2\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}})}^{2}+{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4(4-{t}^{2})}+\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{t}^{2}+4-{t}^{2}}{4(4-{t}^{2})}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}})}{(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}})}=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348429 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347664 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348429 1069 "q-80626" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348429 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348429 1071 "Calculate ${∬}_{R}(2+2x-3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A$,  where $R=[0,3]\\times [0,2].$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348429 1072 #uuid "a3840370-2613-5223-99ab-f0ad1e415b85" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348429 1074 "Using the linearity property of double integrals, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}(2+2x-3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{R}2\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{R}2x\\,\\text{d}A-{∬}_{R}3{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function under the first integral is a constant, so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}2\\,\\text{d}A=2\\,\\text{Area}(R)=2\\cdot (3\\cdot 2)=12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe function under the second integral is independent of $y.$ So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}2x\\,\\text{d}A & =(2-0){\\int }_{0}^{3}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot [{x}^{2}]_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =2({3}^{2}-(0)^{2}) \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function under the third integral is independent of $x.$ So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}2y\\,\\text{d}A & =(3-0){\\int }_{0}^{2}3{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =3\\cdot [{y}^{3}]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =3({2}^{3}-{0}^{3}) \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, combining our results, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}(2+2x-3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A=12+18-24=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348430 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347774 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348430 1069 "q-80627" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348430 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348430 1071 "Evaluate the double integral ${∬}_{R}\\frac{2+6y{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A$,  where $R=[1,2]\\times [2,4].$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348430 1072 #uuid "3c76c336-dd88-5200-b5c5-bdf849ab058b" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348430 1074 "Using the linearity property of double integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}\\frac{2+6y{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{R}(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}+6y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{R}\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{R}6y\\text{ }\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function under the first integral is independent of $y.$ So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & =(4-2){\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-\\frac{2}{x}{|}_{1}^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-\\frac{2}{2}-(-\\frac{2}{1})) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function under the second integral is independent of $x.$ So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}6y\\text{ }\\text{d}A & =(2-1){\\int }_{2}^{4}6y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =3{y}^{2}{|}_{2}^{4} \\\\  & =3({4}^{2}-{2}^{2}) \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, combining our results, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}\\frac{2+6y{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A=2+36=38.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348431 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347629 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348431 1069 "q-8063" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348431 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348431 1071 "Find the asymptotes of the hyperbola $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}=1.$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348431 1072 #uuid "805df202-2726-533c-8d26-9f1ff7771e30" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348431 1074 "The standard equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at the origin is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nFor a horizontal hyperbola centered at the origin, the equations of the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny=\\pm \\frac{b}{a}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\na=\\sqrt{4}=2,\\,b=\\sqrt{16}=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the asymptotes of the hyperbola are \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\pm \\frac{4}{2}x \\\\  & =\\pm 2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348432 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347809 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348432 1069 "q-80634" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348432 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348432 1071 "![](images/a5/a51b5dca6436e6ffd3af0d1cfa801141d97e7660e6218344011a5ac965c722c6.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the curves $y=2(x-2)^{2}-1$ and $y=2-(x-2)^{2},$ as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA=$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348432 1072 #uuid "f422f55c-98bf-59c7-bc1b-bc01ed79cf99" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348432 1074 "First, let's represent $D$ as a type I region. To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/a1/a15535c1eee33018c0486fd3cf1900fd151a9a23cf374c6a0ccec60ca47c64c3.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 3,\\,2(x-2)^{2}-1\\le y\\le 2-(x-2)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{2(x-2)^{2}-1}^{2-(x-2)^{2}}f(x,y)\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}[{\\int }_{2(x-2)^{2}-1}^{2-(x-2)^{2}}f(x,y)\\,dy]dx\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348433 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347695 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348433 1069 "q-80638" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348433 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348433 1071 "Calculate ${∬}_{R}(6{x}^{2}-10{y}^{4})\\text{d}A$,  where $R=[-2,0]\\times [-1,1].$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348433 1072 #uuid "4748dfcc-23f2-5450-96ca-251f5d6080af" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348433 1074 "Using the linearity property of double integrals, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}(6{x}^{2}-10{y}^{4})\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{R}6{x}^{2}\\text{ }\\text{d}A-{∬}_{R}10{y}^{4}\\text{ }\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function under the first integral is independent of $y.$ So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}6{x}^{2}\\text{ }\\text{d}A & =(1-(-1)){\\int }_{-2}^{0}6{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2{x}^{3}{|}_{-2}^{0} \\\\  & =2(2\\cdot {0}^{3}-2\\cdot (-2)^{3}) \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function under the second integral is independent of $x.$ So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}10{y}^{4}\\text{ }\\text{d}A & =(0-(-2)){\\int }_{-1}^{1}10{y}^{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2{y}^{5}{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =2(2\\cdot {1}^{5}-2\\cdot (-1)^{5}) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, combining our results, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}(6{x}^{2}+10{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}A=32-8=24.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348434 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347682 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348434 1069 "q-80643" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348434 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348434 1071 "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Find the values of $a$ and $b$, if \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =a{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+6xy+2x, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =b{x}^{3}y+3{x}^{2}+2, \\\\ {f}_{y}(-1,1) & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348434 1072 #uuid "327ee2b7-dd3c-55c0-8e52-a02eed7e91bf" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348434 1074 "First, we find the mixed partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({f}_{x}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(a{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+6xy+2x) \\\\  & =2a{x}^{2}y+6x+0 \\\\  & =2a{x}^{2}y+6x \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{y}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(b{x}^{3}y+3{x}^{2}+2) \\\\  & =3b{x}^{2}y+6x+0 \\\\  & =3b{x}^{2}y+6x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. Hence, we have the identity\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2a{x}^{2}y+6x=3b{x}^{2}y+6x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of ${x}^{2}y$, we see that $a=\\frac{3b}{2}.$ \n\nNow, since ${f}_{y}(-1,1)=3$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(-1,1) & =3 \\\\ b(-1)^{3}(1)+3(-1)^{2}+2 & =3 \\\\ -b+3+2 & =3 \\\\ b & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na=\\frac{3b}{2}=3.\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348435 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347883 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348435 1069 "q-80646" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348435 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348435 1071 "Determine the stationary point of $f(x,y)=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-4xy+{y}^{2}.$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348435 1072 #uuid "d15295c3-5868-57e4-8227-d3f5fdb96a55" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348435 1074 "The stationary points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $a$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T},$ where\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the domain of $f$ is $D={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}∣x>0},$ and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{2}{x}-4y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-4x+2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{x}-4y & -4x+2y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the stationary points, we need to find the points where ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}.$\nSetting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{x}-4y=0 \\\\ -4x+2y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $y$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{x}-4y=0\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{1}{2x}.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4x+2(\\frac{1}{2x}) & =0 \\\\ -4x+(\\frac{1}{x}) & =0 \\\\ 4x & =\\frac{1}{x}. \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBack-substituting these values into $y=\\frac{1}{2x},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{1}{2} & \\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{1}{2(\\frac{1}{2})}=1 \\\\ x=-\\frac{1}{2} & \\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{1}{2(-\\frac{1}{2})}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our system has two solutions, $(\\frac{1}{2},1)$ and $(-\\frac{1}{2},-1).$ We reject the point $(-\\frac{1}{2},-1)$ because it does not lie in the domain of $f(x,y).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f$ has one stationary point, $(\\frac{1}{2},1).$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348436 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347874 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348436 1069 "q-80647" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348436 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348436 1071 "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Find the values of $a$ and $b$, if \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =ay(1+x){e}^{x}+y{e}^{y}+4, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =x(1+y){e}^{y}-bx{e}^{x}-1, \\\\ {f}_{x}(1,1) & =4+5e.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348436 1072 #uuid "0487a64f-a5c2-5c8e-9d1d-114f7f6f98ad" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348436 1074 "First, we find the mixed partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({f}_{x}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(ay(1+x){e}^{x}+y{e}^{y}+4) \\\\  & =a(1+x){e}^{x}+(1+y){e}^{y} \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{y}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x(1+y){e}^{y}-bx{e}^{x}-1) \\\\  & =(1+y){e}^{y}-b(1+x){e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. Hence, we have the identity\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(1+x){e}^{x}+(1+y){e}^{y}=(1+y){e}^{y}-b(1+x){e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $(1+x){e}^{x},$ respectively, we see that $a=-b.$ \n\nNow, since ${f}_{x}(1,1)=4+5e$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,1) & =4+5e \\\\ a(1)(1+(1))e+(1)e+4 & =4+5e \\\\ 2ae+e+4 & =4+5e \\\\ (2a+1)e & =5e \\\\ 2a+1 & =5 \\\\ a & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\nb=-a=-2.\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348437 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347691 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348437 1069 "q-80657" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348437 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348437 1071 "Given that $C$ and $D$ are finite, non-overlapping regions and that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{C}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A=-5,\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A=7,\n$$\n\nfind the value of \n\n$$\n{∬}_{C\\cup D}4f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A.\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348437 1072 #uuid "dfc30ed8-539a-5f2e-be5b-7fef09f28bdd" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348437 1074 "Using the linearity and additivity of double integrals, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{C\\cup D}4f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A & =4{∬}_{C\\cup D}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A \\\\  & =4({∬}_{C}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A) \\\\  & =4(-5+7) \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348438 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347837 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348438 1069 "q-80661" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348438 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348438 1071 "Given that $C$ and $D$ are finite, non-overlapping regions and that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{C}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A=\\frac{1}{2},\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A=\\frac{3}{2},\n$$\n\nfind the value of \n\n$$\n{∬}_{C\\cup D}\\frac{f(x,y)}{2}\\text{ }\\text{d}A.\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348438 1072 #uuid "c8f1c2d8-aadd-57cc-878c-19c22388e69f" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348438 1074 "Using the linearity and additivity of double integrals, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{C\\cup D}\\frac{f(x,y)}{2}\\text{ }\\text{d}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{∬}_{C\\cup D}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({∬}_{C}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348439 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347974 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348439 1069 "q-80669" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348439 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348439 1071 "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+3x}{{x}^{2}-3x-4}.$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348439 1072 #uuid "45255a84-5494-574a-bd9f-880b4b37a48c" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348439 1074 "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{x(x+3)}{(x+1)(x-4)}.\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)(x-4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the two solutions $x=-1$ and $x=4.$ Therefore, the equations of the vertical asymptotes are $x=-1$ and $x=4.$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348440 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347742 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348440 1069 "q-80676" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348440 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348440 1071 "Calculate the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{2x-1}{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+4x-8}.$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348440 1072 #uuid "6e336039-4f92-550b-b76c-a2a62662e884" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348440 1074 "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2x-1}{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+4x-8} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x-1}{{x}^{2}(x-2)+4(x-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x-1}{({x}^{2}+4)(x-2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+4)(x-2)=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve the above equation using the zero product property:\n\n- Solving $x-2=0$ gives the solution $x=2.$\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}+4=0$ has no real solutions.\n\nTherefore, the only vertical asymptote is $x=2.$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348441 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347705 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348441 1069 "q-80685" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348441 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348441 1071 "Determine the critical points of $f(x,y)={x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}y+6{y}^{2}.$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348441 1072 #uuid "a1fd2252-93e6-52b7-b6db-b0208e66793d" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348441 1074 "The critical points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $a$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ or ${f}^{′}(a)$ does not exist, where\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the domain of $f$ is ${R}^{2},$ and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =3{x}^{2}-6xy \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-3{x}^{2}+12y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-6xy & -3{x}^{2}+12y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points, we need to find the points $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$ such that ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}$ or ${f}^{′}(x,y)$ does not exist. Note that, in our case, ${f}^{′}(x,y)$ exists everywhere, so we just need to find the points where ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}.$\n\nSetting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-6xy=0 \\\\ -3{x}^{2}+12y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $y$ in the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-3{x}^{2}+12y=0\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-6x(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}) & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-\\frac{6{x}^{3}}{4} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(1-\\frac{x}{2}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}(2-x) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBack-substituting these values into $y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=0 & \\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{{0}^{2}}{4}=0 \\\\ x=2 & \\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{{2}^{2}}{4}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our system has two solutions, $(0,0)$ and $(2,1).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f$ has two critical points, $(0,0)$ and $(2,1).$ Note that since ${f}^{′}(x)={0}^{T}$ at these two points, they are also both stationary points." 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348442 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347677 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348442 1069 "q-80691" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348442 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348442 1071 "Calculate the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{x+2}{{x}^{2}+2x}.$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348442 1072 #uuid "3dad69b3-29eb-58b2-9a73-eefa5740f30c" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348442 1074 "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x+2}{{x}^{2}+2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+2}{x(x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{x+2}}{x\\cancel{(x+2)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\nx=0\n$$\n\nThis gives the solution $x=0.$ Therefore, $x=0$ is the only vertical asymptote." 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348443 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347642 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348443 1069 "q-80696" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348443 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348443 1071 "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{4x}{x({x}^{2}+1)}.$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348443 1072 #uuid "d7fbfa1c-696c-569c-8a32-b9f903615eb9" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348443 1074 "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{4x}{x({x}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cancel{x}}{\\cancel{x}({x}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+1=0\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any real solutions. Therefore, $f(x)$ has no vertical asymptotes." 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348444 1068 #edb/ref 17592186348048 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348444 1069 "q-80703" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348444 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348444 1071 "![](images/da/da6318e1b1b8123845993da64cea95f640b0cb67ad94bb183bbbe9e5b1bd7b89.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348444 1072 #uuid "94f5274e-15ab-5af9-b57e-c5cf15e988dc" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348444 1074 "![](images/8f/8f8ffabcc5b03b1f1b5856f4eb187137d1bf84043e47a43f6777df7b267853ec.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=0$ and $y=4,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines. \n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =xy-4x=x(y-4) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =x(y-4)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $x=0$ and $y=4$ divide the plane into $4$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $4$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| $y>4$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<4$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |\n\n \nOf the remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=xy-4x=x(y-4).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=xy-4x.$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348445 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347789 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348445 1069 "q-80714" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348445 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348445 1071 "![](images/fd/fd7e9afcccbbb635d6bde139697aec0cbaa4bad486ea8b098e96f74bfbefebc9.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{y}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{2}{y-1}$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348445 1072 #uuid "50a7324b-9111-57a6-8ac7-64efd93f7ba4" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348445 1074 "![](images/28/28c4751694caf50f08f73d7ccf3525877e6679901d49854951810f550a65ede2.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope lines on the same horizontal line are all the same. This means that the slope does not vary with $x.$ In other words, ${y}^{′}$ is a function of $y$ only.\n\n- Second, the slopes become vertical as $y\\to 1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation I does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{y}$ is a function of $y$ only, but\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{y}\\to 1\\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to 1.\n$$\n\n- Equation II does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x}$ is a function of $x$ only.\n\n- Equation III gives the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=\\frac{2}{y-1}$ is a function of $y$ only, and \n\n$$\n|{y}^{′}|=|\\frac{2}{y-1}|\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348446 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347844 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348446 1069 "q-80723" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348446 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348446 1071 "Given the linear transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}2x-y+3z \\\\ 8x-3y+10z \\\\ -6x+3y-9z\\end{aligned}],$ find $\\text{nullity}(T).$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348446 1072 #uuid "63bbd457-4720-5df6-b92e-bec18dfe5f4b" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348446 1074 "First, we write down the standard matrix of $T:$\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 8 & -3 & 10 \\\\ -6 & 3 & -9\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T)).$\n\nIn order to find $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T)),$ we reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 8 & -3 & 10 \\\\ -6 & 3 & -9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -6 & 3 & -9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has $1$ non-pivot columns (the third one). Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T))=1.\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348447 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347900 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348447 1069 "q-80735" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348447 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348447 1071 "If $A={4,{6,11},5,{7,13,15},{8,2}}$, then $|A|=$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348447 1072 #uuid "3fbf3216-a689-505a-b75a-9d29359894a3" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348447 1074 "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nLet's start by listing all the elements of $A:$\n\n$$\n4,\\,{6,11},\\,5,\\,{7,13,15},\\,{8,2}\n$$\n\nNote that ${6,11}$,  ${7,13,15}$ and ${8,2}$ each count as single elements of $A.$\n\nSince $A$ has $5$ elements, the cardinality is $|A|=5.$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348448 1068 #edb/ref 17592186348043 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348448 1069 "q-80737" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348448 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348448 1071 "What is the cardinality of $A={∅,{∅},{{∅}}}?$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348448 1072 #uuid "67d3f28e-056a-5ab9-83e0-b4d2d7b06a47" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348448 1074 "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nLet's start by listing all the elements of $A:$\n\n$$\n∅,\\,{∅},\\,{{∅}}\n$$\n\nNote that $∅,$ ${∅},$ and ${{∅}}$ each count as single elements of $A.$\n\nSince $A$ has $3$ elements, the cardinality is $|A|=3.$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348449 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347760 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348449 1069 "q-8075" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348449 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348449 1071 "Write the complex number $z=1+2\\text{i}$ in the form $r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ giving $\\theta $ in radians to $3$ decimal places." 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348449 1072 #uuid "14979d9f-0bb7-56e3-a3fd-4077e5759f2a" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348449 1074 "To write $z$ in the form $z=r{\\text{e}}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ where $r=|z|$ and $\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z),$ we first calculate $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $z$ lies in the first quadrant of the complex plane, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{2}{1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2) \\\\  & ≈1.107.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $r=\\sqrt{5}$ and $\\theta =1.107$ into $z=r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ we get\n\n$$\nz=\\sqrt{5}\\,{\\text{e}}^{1.107\\,\\text{i}}.\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348450 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347969 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348450 1069 "q-80753" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348450 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348450 1071 "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=2x{y}^{2}-y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=2{x}^{2}y-2x$\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=yx{e}^{x}+{y}^{2}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=(x-1){e}^{x}+2xy$\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=x-y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}-x$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348450 1072 #uuid "22141837-7250-500e-8f49-e3a949023800" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348450 1074 "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x{y}^{2}-y)=4xy-1 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}y-2x)=4xy-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(yx{e}^{x}+{y}^{2})=x{e}^{x}+2y \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((x-1){e}^{x}+2xy)=x{e}^{x}+2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x-y)=-1 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}-x)=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$,  the third pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II and III only'." 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348451 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347773 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348451 1069 "q-80759" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348451 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348451 1071 "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)={e}^{-2x}-y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)={e}^{-y}-2x$\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=5$\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=2{y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy+{y}^{2}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡xy+2xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy+2xy$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348451 1072 #uuid "0fedf8ed-0c48-5105-b489-f0efda76d273" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348451 1074 "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{-2x}-y)=-1 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{-y}-2x)=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=0 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy+{y}^{2})=4y\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy-2x{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡xy+2y \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡xy+2xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy+2xy) \\\\  & =2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy+2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy-2x{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡xy+2y \\\\  & =4y\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy-2x{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡xy+2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$,  the third pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II and III only'." 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348452 1068 #edb/ref 17592186348013 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348452 1069 "q-8076" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348452 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348452 1071 "Write the complex number $z=5-\\text{i}$ in the form $r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ giving $\\theta $ in radians to $3$ decimal places." 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348452 1072 #uuid "0a752e22-d98b-55fa-871d-a83c493259aa" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348452 1074 "To write our complex number in the form $z=r{\\text{e}}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ where $r=|z|$ and $\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z),$ we first calculate $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{26}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant, we can find the angle $\\theta $ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =2\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})| \\\\  & =2\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{-1}{5})| \\\\  & =2\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{1}{5})| \\\\  & ≈2\\pi -0.197\\,39… \\\\  & ≈6.086.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $r=\\sqrt{26}$ and $\\theta =6.086$ into $z=r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ we get\n\n$$\nz=\\sqrt{26}\\,{\\text{e}}^{6.086\\,\\text{i}}.\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348453 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347869 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348453 1069 "q-80764" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348453 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348453 1071 "Given the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{3}{y}^{2}+y{z}^{2}$, find the equation of the tangent plane to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,1).$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348453 1072 #uuid "637ec3e0-24b1-5de9-b7ed-f540f4d79ea6" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348453 1074 "The equation of the tangent plane to the level surface $f(x,y,z)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})=(1,-1,1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}{y}^{2}+y{z}^{2})\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}{y}^{2}+y{z}^{2})\\text{ }j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{3}{y}^{2}+y{z}^{2})\\text{ }k \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\text{ }i+(2y{x}^{3}+{z}^{2})\\text{ }j+2yzk \\\\ ∇\\,f(1,-1,1) & =3(1)^{2}(-1)^{2}\\text{ }i+(\\,2(-1)(1)^{3}+(1)^{2}\\,)\\text{ }j+2(-1)(1)k \\\\  & =3\\,i-1\\,j-2k \\\\  & =⟨3,-1,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write down the Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the level surface, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(1,-1,1)\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y+1,\\,z-1⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨3,-1,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y+1,\\,z-1⟩ & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348454 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347960 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348454 1069 "q-80767" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348454 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348454 1071 "Given the function $f(x,y,z)=xy-yz-xz$, find the equation of the tangent plane to the level surface that passes  through the point where $(x,y,z)=(2,3,4).$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348454 1072 #uuid "bbcded61-18ec-5362-9f48-c67e58a60e0a" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348454 1074 "The equation of the tangent plane to the level surface $f(x,y,z)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})=(2,3,4):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy-yz-xz)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy-yz-xz)\\text{ }j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xy-yz-xz)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =(y-z)\\text{ }i+(x-z)\\text{ }j+(-y-x)k \\\\ ∇\\,f(2,3,4) & =(3-4)\\text{ }i+(2-4)\\text{ }j+(-3-2)k \\\\  & =-1\\text{ }i-2\\text{ }j-5k \\\\  & =⟨-1,-2,-5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe can now write down the Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the level surface, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(2,3,4)\\cdot ⟨x-2,\\,y-3,\\,z-4⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨-1,-2,-5⟩\\cdot ⟨x-2,\\,y-3,\\,z-4⟩ & =0 \\\\ -(x-2)-2(y-3)-5(z-4) & =0 \\\\ -x-2y-5z+28 & =0 \\\\ x+2y+5z-28 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348455 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347706 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348455 1069 "q-80769" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348455 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348455 1071 "Given the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+2{z}^{2}$,  find the equation of the tangent plane to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(2,1,1).$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348455 1072 #uuid "4798bf87-b861-5528-ad8e-12b959ab1ff8" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348455 1074 "The equation of the tangent plane to the level surface $f(x,y,z)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇\\,f({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})=(2,1,1):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+2{z}^{2})\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+2{z}^{2})\\text{ }j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+2{z}^{2})\\text{ }k \\\\  & =2x\\text{ }i+8y\\text{ }j+4z\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇\\,f(2,1,1) & =2(2)\\text{ }i+8(1)j+4(1)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =4\\,i+8\\,j+4\\,k \\\\  & =⟨4,8,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe can now write down the Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the level surface, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇\\,f(2,1,1)\\cdot ⟨x-2,\\,y-1,\\,z-1⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨4,8,4⟩\\cdot ⟨x-2,\\,y-1,\\,z-1⟩ & =0 \\\\ 4(x-2)+8(y-1)+4(z-1) & =0 \\\\ 4x+8y+4z-20 & =0 \\\\ x+2y+z-5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348456 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347707 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348456 1069 "q-80778" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348456 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348456 1071 "Consider the surface ${2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy}=2.$ Given that $n=⟨a,b,c⟩$ is a non-zero normal vector to the surface at the point $(1,-1,1),$ what is the value of $\\frac{c}{b}?$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348456 1072 #uuid "581af8f1-8855-519d-a20e-429c65cef0c0" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348456 1074 "Let's define  $f(x,y,z)={2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy}.$ Then, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=2.$  \n\nNow, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $(1,-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy})i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy})j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy})k \\\\  & =({2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy}\\cdot 2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)i+({2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy}\\cdot 2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)j+({2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy}\\cdot 6z\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)k \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2(y\\,\\text{ }{2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy}\\text{ }i+x\\,\\text{ }{2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy}\\text{ }j+3z\\,\\text{ }{2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy}\\text{ }k) \\\\ ∇f(1,-1,1) & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2((-1)\\cdot \\text{ }{2}^{3(1)^{2}+2\\cdot 1\\cdot (-1)}\\text{ }i+1\\cdot \\,\\text{ }{2}^{3(1)^{2}+2\\cdot 1\\cdot (-1)}\\text{ }j+3(1)\\cdot {2}^{3(1)^{2}+2\\cdot 1\\cdot (-1)}\\text{ }k) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2(-{2}^{1}\\text{ }i+{2}^{1}\\text{ }j+3\\cdot {2}^{1}\\text{ }k) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2(-i+j+3\\text{ }k) \\\\  & =⟨-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,12\\operatorname{ln}⁡2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our normal vector is $n=⟨a,b,c⟩=⟨-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,12\\operatorname{ln}⁡2⟩.$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{c}{b}=\\frac{12\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}=3.$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348457 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347680 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348457 1069 "q-8078" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348457 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348457 1071 "Write the complex number $z=3\\sqrt{3}+3\\text{i}$ in exponential form." 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348457 1072 #uuid "c929adfb-38d7-5819-8554-7aa009ddd6f8" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348457 1074 "To write $z$ in the form $z=r{\\text{e}}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ where $r=|z|$ and $\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z),$ we first calculate $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3\\sqrt{3})^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36}=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $z$ lies in the first quadrant of the complex plane, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{3}{3\\sqrt{3}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $r=6$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ into $z=r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ we get\n\n$$\nz=6\\,{\\text{e}}^{\\pi \\text{i}/6}.\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348458 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347940 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348458 1069 "q-80783" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348458 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348458 1071 "Consider the surface $z{x}^{3}=-1+x{y}^{2}.$ Find the equation of the tangent plane to that surface at the point $(1,-2,3).$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348458 1072 #uuid "31b12878-697c-50f6-b490-545a54f9c386" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348458 1074 "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z{x}^{3} & =-1+x{y}^{2} \\\\ z{x}^{3}-x{y}^{2} & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z)=z{x}^{3}-x{y}^{2}$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=-1.$ \n\nRecall that the equation of the tangent plane to the level surface $f(x,y,z)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $(1,-2,3):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(z{x}^{3}-x{y}^{2})\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(z{x}^{3}-x{y}^{2})\\text{ }j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(z{x}^{3}-x{y}^{2})\\text{ }k \\\\  & =(3{x}^{2}z-{y}^{2})\\text{ }i-2xy\\text{ }j+{x}^{3}\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇\\,f(1,-2,3) & =(3(1)^{2}(3)-(-2)^{2})\\text{ }i-2(1)(-2)\\text{ }j+(1)^{3}\\text{ }k \\\\  & =5\\,i+4\\,j+1\\,k \\\\  & =⟨5,4,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we can now write down the Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the level surface, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(1,-2,3)\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-(-2),\\,z-3⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨5,4,1⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y+2,\\,z-3⟩ & =0 \\\\ 5(x-1)+4(y+2)+(z-3) & =0 \\\\ 5x+4y+z & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348459 1068 #edb/ref 17592186348002 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348459 1069 "q-8079" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348459 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348459 1071 "Write the complex number $z=1-\\text{i}\\sqrt{3}$ in exponential form." 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348459 1072 #uuid "df18ce8e-40b6-5c6f-8d8d-e5b04d36232b" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348459 1074 "To write our complex number in the form $z=r{\\text{e}}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ where $r=|z|$ and $\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z),$ we first calculate $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-\\sqrt{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant, we can find the angle $\\theta $ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =2\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})| \\\\  & =2\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\sqrt{3})| \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $r=2$ and $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ into $z=r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ we get\n\n$$\nz=2\\,{\\text{e}}^{5\\pi \\text{i}/3}.\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348460 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347736 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348460 1069 "q-8080" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348460 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348460 1071 "![](images/6e/6e2a47a4563e404bc09f285af6ffcb098ea16a49f9c45e450a70ef0cb54f6263.png)\n\nWrite the complex number $z$, shown on the Argand diagram above, in exponential form." 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348460 1072 #uuid "8b44c38e-c4aa-5f62-958f-79e3b1712d3f" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true] [17592186348460 1074 "The complex number shown in the Argand diagram can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =x+\\text{i}y \\\\  & =-3+3\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo write it in the form $z=r{\\text{e}}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ where $r=|z|$ and $\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z),$ we first calculate $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{18} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $z$ lies in the second quadrant of the complex plane, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})| \\\\  & =\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-1)| \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $r=3\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ into $z=r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ we get\n\n$$\nz=3\\sqrt{2}\\,{\\text{e}}^{3\\pi \\text{i}/4}.\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045585 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-00055a40-3e21-5f4f-a9ae-8d1688e527ad" 17592186345359 "answer-001a90bf-ee96-5681-bcb6-3ecb2b28efc4" 17592186345360 "answer-001dc7ef-771a-5f18-a040-35f54dd30558" 17592186345361 "answer-004712b6-f610-5c1b-85fc-ea7f5dac42b6" 17592186345362 "answer-007ed2e6-bd51-5024-8a95-96aa09f05e0f" 17592186345363 "answer-00941107-e9c6-5933-9b3b-706a63234ba7" 17592186345364 "answer-009c9900-f889-52f9-b00b-40100c9397c7" 17592186345365 "answer-00e011e8-4a7a-508d-bca5-385ded8